 "id": "2026-10-23-europe-heat-deaths",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Heat may have killed fifty thousand people in Europe this summer. Why so many?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Two studies in Nature Health, from Imperial College London and the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, estimate fifty thousand, three hundred ninety-one heat deaths in eight hundred fifty-four European cities, from June to August."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "About thirty-two thousand six hundred of those deaths are linked to extra heat from burning fossil fuels, the researchers say. Climate change's share rose from about forty percent early this century to sixty-five percent this summer."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "Italy has the highest estimated toll, at thirteen thousand three hundred, then France, Spain and Germany. People aged eighty-five and over make up forty-five percent. The cities studied hold about forty percent of the population in thirty countries. The authors say these are modeled estimates, probably an undercount."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will Europe see another summer this deadly? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
